1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,317
This is CrowdPulse, from the inside.

2
00:00:02,317 --> 00:00:04,097
On the right, the app, running.

3
00:00:04,097 --> 00:00:06,394
On the left, the code that’s running it.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:11,701
This isn’t a line by line walkthrough.

5
00:00:11,701 --> 00:00:12,352
It’s a map:

6
00:00:12,352 --> 00:00:16,618
how the code is laid out, and what runs when the app does its main jobs.

7
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:19,955
Start with the layout.

8
00:00:19,955 --> 00:00:23,583
Everything the browser loads is in the public folder:

9
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:26,800
six plain HTML pages, with one script for each.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,948
No framework, and the build just copies files.

11
00:00:30,849 --> 00:00:35,359
Every page loads the same two helpers before its own script.

12
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:40,171
common.js draws the shared pieces, like the gauge and the chart.

13
00:00:40,171 --> 00:00:43,329
supa.js is the connection to the back end.

14
00:00:44,229 --> 00:00:48,508
The back end is Supabase, and almost all of it is one SQL file:

15
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:52,855
the tables, the security rules, and a function for every action.

16
00:00:52,855 --> 00:00:55,301
There’s no server of our own to run.

17
00:00:56,729 --> 00:01:00,637
One idea holds it all together, and it lives in supa.js.

18
00:01:00,637 --> 00:01:06,222
Every page asks for a connection, and gets back a small socket with two methods.

19
00:01:07,229 --> 00:01:08,833
Emit sends something up.

20
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:11,374
Each name maps to a database function:

21
00:01:11,374 --> 00:01:15,186
join, an admin action, or anything else an attendee does.

22
00:01:16,229 --> 00:01:18,678
And on listens for what comes back down:

23
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:23,333
a Realtime broadcast, on a private channel that belongs to this one session.

24
00:01:24,729 --> 00:01:27,517
Up through a function, down through a broadcast.

25
00:01:27,517 --> 00:01:30,596
Every feature in the app is a variation on that loop.

26
00:01:32,229 --> 00:01:33,378
Watch it happen.

27
00:01:33,378 --> 00:01:36,754
An attendee types a join code on the home page.

28
00:01:36,754 --> 00:01:42,789
home.js asks the database whether that session exists, then opens the attendee page.

29
00:01:43,689 --> 00:01:46,624
There, event.js connects and emits join.

30
00:01:46,624 --> 00:01:52,200
In the database, cp_join records the participant, and hands back a snapshot:

31
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,209
everything the page needs to draw itself.

32
00:01:56,109 --> 00:01:59,557
From then on, the page keeps that snapshot as its state,

33
00:01:59,557 --> 00:02:02,267
and render functions turn state into screen.

34
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:05,346
The session hasn’t started, so it draws the lobby.

35
00:02:06,609 --> 00:02:07,448
Now the host.

36
00:02:07,448 --> 00:02:11,196
In the control room, the Go live button emits admin start.

37
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:18,283
In the database, cp_admin checks that the caller really is an admin of this session,

38
00:02:18,283 --> 00:02:22,410
flips the status to live, and broadcasts the new event to everyone.

39
00:02:24,109 --> 00:02:28,694
On the phone, the event listener sees the status change and renders again.

40
00:02:28,694 --> 00:02:30,924
The lobby becomes the rating screen.

41
00:02:30,924 --> 00:02:32,535
Nobody refreshed anything.

42
00:02:33,609 --> 00:02:34,927
Here’s the heart of it.

43
00:02:34,927 --> 00:02:39,112
Dragging the slider calls send rating, which waits a quarter of a second,

44
00:02:39,112 --> 00:02:40,030
then emits rate.

45
00:02:42,609 --> 00:02:47,819
cp_act routes it to cp_do_rate, which stores the number against that participant.

46
00:02:47,819 --> 00:02:49,492
Notice what it doesn’t do:

47
00:02:49,492 --> 00:02:50,972
it doesn’t tell anyone.

48
00:02:52,609 --> 00:02:54,390
That’s the job of cp_tick.

49
00:02:54,390 --> 00:02:58,707
Every two seconds, the control room and the big screen call it.

50
00:02:58,707 --> 00:03:02,270
It averages the latest ratings, measures the energy,

51
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:04,531
and broadcasts one small message:

52
00:03:04,531 --> 00:03:05,217
the pulse.

53
00:03:06,609 --> 00:03:08,868
Every screen is listening for it.

54
00:03:08,868 --> 00:03:12,360
On pulse updates the gauge, the bars and the chart,

55
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:15,441
all drawn by the shared helpers in common.js.

56
00:03:17,109 --> 00:03:22,442
So a hundred people dragging sliders still means just one broadcast every two seconds.

57
00:03:24,109 --> 00:03:28,431
Chat, questions, polls and reactions all take the same route.

58
00:03:28,431 --> 00:03:33,816
One line to emit, one branch in cp_act, and one listener to draw the result.

59
00:03:36,109 --> 00:03:39,271
And the rules live in the database, not the browser.

60
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:40,609
The tables are closed.

61
00:03:40,609 --> 00:03:44,988
A browser can only call the functions granted at the bottom of the file,

62
00:03:44,988 --> 00:03:47,117
and each one checks who is calling.

63
00:03:49,609 --> 00:03:52,635
When the session ends, there’s one more path.

64
00:03:52,635 --> 00:03:55,796
report.js asks an Edge Function for the report.

65
00:03:57,609 --> 00:04:00,829
The function checks that you’re allowed to see it,

66
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:04,243
reads the raw data with a key the browser never gets,

67
00:04:04,243 --> 00:04:09,139
and runs it through analytics.js, which turns ratings and chat into a grade,

68
00:04:09,139 --> 00:04:10,750
key moments and insights.

69
00:04:13,109 --> 00:04:17,504
Only crowd-level results come back, and Chart.js draws them.

70
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:20,392
So that’s the map.

71
00:04:20,392 --> 00:04:22,532
Six pages, with a script each.

72
00:04:22,532 --> 00:04:23,958
One connection file.

73
00:04:23,958 --> 00:04:26,383
One SQL file that holds the rules.

74
00:04:26,383 --> 00:04:28,665
And one function for the report.

75
00:04:30,609 --> 00:04:35,450
To explore it yourself, run npm run dev, open a session’s control room,

76
00:04:35,450 --> 00:04:38,313
and start the built-in audience simulator.

77
00:04:38,313 --> 00:04:41,382
Then follow one emit, and see where it lands.
